Bio-nanocomposite-based packaging materials have gained significance due to their possible applications in food packaging. Cellulose acetate is a biopolymer obtained by acetylation of cellulose and has characteristics such as biocompatibility, biodegradability and high transparency. Introducing iron-based metal-organic frameworks offer good mechanical strength, unique surface area and both chemical and thermally stability, making them favourable as supporting materials in fabricating polymer-based packaging materials. Among them, Fe- (Material Institute Lavoisier) MIL-88A is an iron-based nontoxic metal-organic framework, integrated with cellulose ace­ tate and spinach extract was added to the prepared material in different compositions and cast as film. The Spinach loaded, Fe- (Material Institute Lavoisier) MIL-88A integrated cellulose acetate film significantly enhanced the tensile strength, water vapour permeability, and anti-microbial activity. The prepared film is then characterized using a scanning electron microscope (SEM), Fourier transforms infrared spectrometer (FTIR), Xray diffraction (XRD) and thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). Further, studies on mechanical properties as well as degradation tests and real-time applications of the prepared films were carried out.
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, bacterial contamination of food has become a major source of concern. To address these concerns and problems, a new class of environmentally friendly and biodegradable packaging materials has emerged. One of the possibilities for reducing human influence on the environment is the creation of biodegradable materials [1]. For the past few decades, several bio-based materials and their novel applications in food packaging have received a lot of attention [2]. Most of the polymers have been successfully used in commercial packaging applications although they are not miscible, for example, high-impact polystyrene (HIPS), the acrylonitrile–butadiene–styrene terpolymer (ABS), etc. which are mechanically incompatible and thermodynamically immis­ cible [3,4]. But having Biodegradable polymers such as cellulose acetate (CA), methylcellulose (MC), hydroxypropylmethylcellulose (HPMC), polyester, pectin, starch, chitosan, poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA), etc. [5,6] have emerged. Out of which CA is the most interesting of all the cellulose derivatives. It is water-insoluble, which is an important property for packaging applications. Furthermore, it has a wide range of applica­ tions, such as reverse osmosis membranes, paper coatings, filters, etc. [7].
However, CA has significant drawbacks, which include low ductility and flexibility, and poor water vapour barrier properties, which all prohibit their application in the food packaging industry. Nanofillers like nano clay, inorganic nanoparticles, carbon nanotubes, and graphene oxide nanosheets, etc. can be incorporated into CA to enhance its me­ chanical and functional properties [8]. Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a broad category of hybrid organic-inorganic supra­ molecules made up of metal ions connected by organic bridge ligands. MOFs are ideal candidates for a wide range of applications, including catalysis, energy storage, bio-imaging, medication delivery, food pack­ aging, etc. due to appealing features like high surface area, acceptable biocompatibility, biodegradability, flexible porosity, etc. MOFs like (Material Institute Lavoisier) MIL-88A, ZIF-67, MOF-1, ZIF-7, MOF-2, ZIF-8, and have been reported in various studies [9–12]. Fe- (Material Institute Lavoisier) MIL-88A (Fe-MIL-88A) is one of the most promising materials in these MOFs. Fe-MIL-88A has a three-dimensional frame­ work with interconnected pores and cages, consisting of oxo-centred trimers of iron (III) octahedrally connected by fumarate linkers with high flexibility. Since both constituents are naturally present in the human body, the regular hexagonal-shaped particles in this material are biocompatible and nontoxic [13]. The main objective of this work was to develop a high-performance and biodegradable packaging material with high mechanical properties. Therefore, to overcome the drawbacks of CA it was reinforced with Fe-MIL-88A to enhance the above-mentioned properties. The influence of MOF on the CA/Fe-MIL-88A composite was evaluated by studying the structural, thermal and mechanical perfor­ mance of the nanocomposites. According to recent studies, introducing an adequate amount of antimicrobial agent (AM) into polymer films used for packaging will effectively suppress bacterial growth that causes food to rot. In that case, AM packaging plays a major role in inhibiting the growth of specific microorganisms in foods, improving food safety and extending shelf life without sacrificing food quality [14].
Plants are the cheapest and safest alternative sources of antimicro­ bials and are used in treatments for both phytochemical and antibac­ terial activity. Allmania nodiflora spinach, a widespread herb and green leafy vegetable species in the Amaranthaceae family is commonly known as celosia. Allmania nodiflora spinach extract (ANE) contains a mixture of alkaloids, flavonoids, saponins, glycosides, steroids and polyphenols, these have antibacterial, phytochemical, fungicidal, and antiviral capabilities. ANE is robust against both Gram-positive and Gram-negative microorganisms [15]. However, the Allmania nodiflora spinach extract-loaded MOF/CA matrix has not been reported in the literature. Hence a spinach-loaded MOF/CA composite film was pre­ pared as the MOF increases the film’s mechanical properties while spinach increases its antibacterial activity. Spinach extract was well dispersed in MOF/CA composite film due to its hydrophilicity. With the addition of ANE to the CA/MOF matrix, the MOF-based degradable films were successfully prepared against $E$ . coli and S. aureus. The ability of films to prolong the shelf life of food products was tested and it proved to be a novel biodegradable material with antibacterial activity.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
Cellulose acetate (CA, $39.8~\mathrm{wt\%}$ of acetyl content, $\mathbf{Mnca.}=30\mathbf{,}000$ $\mathbf{g}{\cdot}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{o}\mathbf{l}^{-1}$ , ${\tt D S}=2.45\mathrm{\partial}$ , ferric chloride hexahydrate, fumaric acid, acetone, and sodium hydroxide, DMF was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich, the selected medicinal plant Allmania nodiflora spinach was collected from the local market in Chinnalapatti, Dindigul district, Tamil Nadu.
## 2.2. Synthesis of Fe-MIL 88A
Hydrothermal synthesis was followed, wherein $0.9744\mathrm{~g~}$ fumaric acid and $2.2722\mathrm{g}\mathrm{FeCl_{3}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ were added to a beaker containing $50~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water. The mixed solutions were stirred for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ with a magnetic stirrer before being placed in a $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ Teflon-lined steel autoclave and heated at $105^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The precipitates were recovered by centrifugation at $9000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ and cooled until they reached room temperature. To remove any excess fumaric acid that did not participate in the reactions, the as-prepared solid was washed with ethanol and water several times. After centrifugation, the final pre­ cipitates were dried in a vacuum oven at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for at least $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ [16].
## 2.3. Extraction of Allmania nodiflora spinach
$50~\mathrm{g}$ Allmania nodiflora spinach leaves were washed with water until all the dust particles were removed and then dried. The dried leaves were ground to a fine powder, which was soaked in $200\mathrm{ml}$ of ethanol in a $500\mathrm{ml}$ beaker for about $24\mathrm{h}$ . After that, the crude sample was filtered through the Whatman filter paper. The collected sample was concen­ trated in a rotary evaporator [17].
## 2.4. Preparation of spinach-loaded CA/MOF matrix film
As follows, composite films were produced using a modified method as shown in Fig. 1. [18]. By dissolving cellulose acetate in acetone, the casting solution was prepared. The preceding solution was stirred at ambient temperature to attain a homogeneous solution using a magnetic stirrer. Then, a $1\%$ solution of Fe-MIL 88A dispersed in acetone was mixed with CA and stirred for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to obtain a CA/Fe-MIL 88A mixture. Different amounts of spinach extract relative to the total mass of the CA/ Fe-MIL 88A mixture were also added and mixed to produce various concentrations of spinach extract in the films. A casting instrument has been used to uniformly cast a homogeneous spinach extract-loaded CA/ Fe-MIL 88A film-forming solution. The obtained film was placed in a coagulant wash & dried to form composite films. The films were kept for $24\mathrm{h}$ in a humidifier at ambient temperature with a relative humidity of $50\%$ for further use. The composed films that had been prepared were assigned names: CA(F1), CA/Fe-MIL-88A(F2), 2 mL of ANE-CA/Fe-MIL 88A(F3), $4~\mathrm{mL}$ of ANE-CA/Fe-MIL 88A(F4), 6 mL of ANE-CA/Fe-MIL
88A(F5), 8 mL of ANE-CA/Fe-MIL 88A(F6), 10 mL of ANE-CA/Fe-MIL   
88A(F7).
## 2.5. Characterization details and analysis
Functional groups in CA, Fe-MIL 88A and Allmania nodiflora extractreinforced composite films were analyzed using an FTIR (Fourier Transform Infrared Spectrometer) on a JASCO FTIR – 460 Plus, in the $4000{-}400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ range. The morphologies of bare CA (F1), C A/Fe-MIL 88A (F2) and $2~\mathrm{mL}$ of ANE-CA/Fe-MIL 88A (F3), 4 mL (F4), 6 mL (F5) of ANE-CA/Fe-MIL 88A, films were investigated by SEM. XRD charac­ terization was performed to evaluate the crystal structure of Fe-MIL 88A. The prepared films’ thermal stability was analyzed using A SEIKO model TG/DTA 6200 being used for TGA To test the thermal stability of the prepared films, a SEIKO model TG/DTA 6200 was used. The prepared films’ water contact angles were measured by goniometry (rame - hart instrument. Co, USA). The average of three measurements was used to measure each composite film’s water contact angle. To investigate the mechanical strength of composite films, a universal testing machine (UTM, Tec Sol India) was used at $23^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ ASTM D638 with a crosshead speed of $5\ \mathrm{mm/min}$ . After measurement and determination of tensile strength, the tensile index was calculated. The tensile index is given by tensile strength per basic weight, and the unit is $\mathrm{{Nm/g}}$ [19]. Testing was performed with test films having a thickness of $150~\mathrm{mm}\times25~\mathrm{mm}$ (in mm) dimension [20]. The composite film’s thickness was measured using a digital screw gauge at five distinct places, and the average values were given as the thickness values for the films.
## 2.5.1. Water vapour permeability
A slight modification of the standard procedure E96-E95 (ASTM method 1995) [21] has been used to assess the water vapour perme­ ability (WVP) of films. The sample films were firmly placed on the head of a $15\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ glass vial, and Calcium chloride anhydrous was pre-weighed using a standard procedure. The vials were retained at $75\pm5\%$ RH and kept in a desiccator with a saturated sodium chloride solution at $30\pm$ $2{\mathsf{\Omega}}^{\circ}\mathbf{C}$ . In the desiccator, which was tightly closed, the vials were measured before and after 1 day. The film’s WVP was calculated based on the formula Eq. (1),
$$
\mathrm{WVP}={\frac{\mathrm{A}\varDelta\mathrm{x}}{({\tt p}_{1}-{\tt p}_{2})\mathrm{S}}}
$$
where A $(\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{h})$ is the slope of the linear regression of the weight in time, Δx (mm) is the film thickness, $({\tt p}1-{\tt p}2)$ (kPa) is the difference in partial pressures inside and outside the cell, and S $(\mathbf{m}^{2})$ is the surface area of the film.
## 2.5.2. Transparency of the film
A UV–vis spectrophotometer was used to analyze the polymeric films’ absorbance between 280 and $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, to determine their transparency. The test film was divided into rectangular pieces and put in a sample container for the spectrophotometric technique. The film’s opacity was determined according to the method in Eq. (2),
$$
\mathrm{T}=\mathrm{Abs}_{600}/\mathrm{X}
$$
where T denotes transparency, Abs600 refers to the film’s absorbance around $600\ \mathrm{nm}$ , while $\mathtt{X}$ represents the film’s thickness in mm [22].
## 2.5.3. Biodegradation tests
Biodegradation tests were carried out as described in the literature [23]. Regarding that, films that were free from moisture were taken as $(\mathsf{W}_{0})$ . The weighted films (F1, F2, F3, F4, and F5) were placed onto the soil for 60 days in a squeezed atmosphere. The aforementioned setup was maintained at room temperature following regular water additions. Changes over time were gravimetrically measured. Films that were already deposited were cleaned properly. Afterwards, all films were reweighed $(\mathsf{W_{t}})$ to estimate the average weight reduction $(\%\mathrm{W_{L}})$ [24]. To find $\%$ of weight reduction $\mathsf{W_{L}}$ values using Eq. (3) the preceding methods were performed three times.
$$
\%\mathrm{{W_{L}}=\frac{\partial W_{o}-W_{t}}{\partial W_{o}}X100}
$$
## 2.5.4. Antibacterial activity of food packaging films
The antibacterial activity of films was evaluated using the diffusion method against Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus) and Escherichia coli (E. coli) [25]. Before testing, the samples were sterilized in nutrient agar. Following sterilization, the produced mixture was poured onto Petri plates and allowed to solidify. Sample microorganisms $5\%$ of the lower inoculum) were scrubbed on the surface of the solidified agar plate once it had solidified. All films were clipped into $10\mathrm{mm}$ -diameter circle parts, which were then placed on an inoculation agar plate and incubated for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at ${37}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ After incubation, the microbial inhibition distance was measured.
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Mechanical properties
TS (tensile strength) and EAB (elongation at break) results of different samples are shown in Fig. 2. TS and EAB values of F1 (CA) film were $5.47~\mathrm{MPa}$ and $22.55~\%$ , respectively. By gradually increasing the amount of antibacterial agent as $2\mathrm{mL}$ , $4\mathrm{mL}$ and $6\mathrm{mL}$ for the films F3, F4 and F5 respectively, along with MIL 88A, increased the TS of composite film from $15.9\ \mathrm{\MPa}$ to $22.78\mathrm{\MPa}$ whereas EAB values gradually declined from $20.57~\%$ to $7.94~\%$ . The results suggest that Fe-MIL 88A MOF is a highly effective reinforcing agent for the CA matrix and its overall tensile strength and corresponding index values are shown in Table 1. Moreover, the good dispersion of Fe-MIL 88A MOF and the good interfacial adhesion between Cu-MOF and CA suppress phase separation [26,27]. The strong binding nature of CA/MIL 88A and the antibacterial agent could probably be the reason for the change in mechanical properties. As a result of the presence of $\mathtt{M I L88A}$ with an antibacterial agent, the molecules could have been packed densely in order to enhance tensile strength by acting as a cross-linker. On the other hand, the composite film’s flexibility was decreased which is reflected in reduced EAB values. The addition of an antibacterial agent up to $6~\mathrm{mL}$ did not cause a considerable change in mechanical properties, which further enhanced the antibacterial agent affected the tensile strength of composite films, due to the higher hydrophilicity of films F6 and F7. So the optimized ratio of the antibacterial agent is fixed at $6~\mathrm{mL}$ (F5) film. Similar reinforcing effects were also observed in other CA-based nano­ composite films, such as $\mathrm{CA}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and CA/polysaccharides composite films. For instance, the tensile strength value reported by Ying Gao et al. [28] increased from 4.29 to $7.15~\mathrm{{\MPa}}$ with $4~\mathrm{wt}\%$ $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ loading compared with the case of the CA film. Rajeswari, et al. [29] reported that the incorporation of polysaccharides into the CA matrix increased the tensile strength to $19.4\mathrm{MPa}$ . By comparing with relevant results, it is obvious that the reinforcing effect of Fe-MIL 88A/ANE on the composite film increased the tensile strength value from $5.4~\mathrm{MPa}$ to $22.78~\mathrm{MPa}$ with $1\mathrm{\Deltaw\%}$ Fe-MIL 88A loading CA which are comparatively having higher tensile strength value with that of the literature referred above. The improved mechanical properties of the CA/Fe-MIL 88A/ANE nanocomposite film make it more promising for practical application.
## 3.2. SEM
The morphology of the prepared film was studied using SEM and the image of CA (F1) film Fig. 3a. indicated a slightly porous and cracked nature. Whereas, Fig. 3b. Fe-MIL 88A exhibits a regular hexagonal rodlike structure. Fe-MIL 88A well embedded onto the CA film as seen in Fig. 3c. While Fig. 3d, e and f respectively of films F3, F4 and F5 show the different ratios $2{\mathrm{~mL}}$ , $4~\mathrm{mL}$ , $6~\mathrm{mL}$ ) of ANE antibacterial agent blended with CA/Fe-MIL 88A mixture exhibited different morphologies like smooth, flat surface and without of pores which were due to the fascinating compatibility of ANE in the CA/Fe – MIL 88A matrix. Hence, the prepared Fe-MIL 88A and Antibacterial agents like Allmania nodi­ flora extract were well mixed and dispersed onto the CA film. SEM im­ ages and corresponding EDAX mapping images show only the presence of Fe, C and O elements in the F3 film [30,31]. It can be concluded that Fe – MIL 88A functioned as the nanocage carrier conveying ANE and significantly improve the dispersion of ANE without affecting the film structure. SEM images and corresponding EDX images show only the presence of C, Fe and O elements in the F3 composite film proving that Fe-MIL 88A/ANE was evenly distributed in the composite films and no other impurities were present.
## 3.3. FTIR
In the F1 spectrum illustrated in Fig. 4 peaks for functional groups $\mathtt{C=}0$ , OH, $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , and ether $_{\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{O}-\mathsf{C}}$ and identified at 1734, 3453, 1369/ 1219, and $1029~\mathrm{{cm}}~^{-1}$ respectively. This is supported by earlier data [29]. Peaks in the Fe-MIL-88A spectrum are slightly shifted to higher wavenumbers and a new peak at $551~\mathrm{{cm}}^{\ -1}$ is assigned to Fe–O stretching in the Fe-MIL-88A structure. The formation of this bond demonstrates that the organic ligands are attached to the inorganic metal of the cluster showing the successful synthesis of the Fe-MIL 88A [32]. The absorption peaks around 1390 and $1549~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively, are attributed to the carboxyl’s vibration modes in the fumarate as the bridging ligand of Fe-MIL-88A [33]. Absorption peaks of all reinforced composites (F2, F3, F4, F5) with $1\%$ Fe-MIL 88A and ANE loading remain the same, showing that the chemical structure of the films is unchanged by the addition of Fe-MIL 88A and ANE. The broad and bending vibration of the hydrogen bond $(\mathrm{-oH}$ group) of CA occurs at $3500{-}3000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ due to the stretching frequency of water. Increasing antibacterial agents in composite films causes no obvious difference in the position of peaks at different spectra, suggesting that Fe – MIL 88A/ ANE does not undergo any change in the chemical structure of the CA films. Increasing antibacterial agents in composite films causes no discernible differences in the position of peaks at different spectra, showing that the MOF-integrated antibacterial agent also does not affect the film’s functional groups.
## 3.4. XRD
The XRD patterns of the Fe-MIL 88A, F1, F2, F3, F4, and F5 com­ posite films as synthesized are shown in Fig. 5. For Fe-MIL 88A, all bands are consistent with previous literature [34], confirming that the pre­ pared Fe-MOF is pure and crystalline in nature. Furthermore, two peaks at around $10.2^{\circ}$ and $11.8^{\circ}$ could be attributed to (100) and (101) planes. For pure CA (F1) film, one weak diffraction peak observed at $8.7^{\circ}$ for F1 film corresponds to the semi-crystalline acetylated cellulose [35]. XRD spectra of CA/Fe-MIL 88A composites show Fe-MIL 88A characteristic peaks in the F2 film as well as the F3, F4, and F5 films within Fe-MIL 88A’s peak, proving Fe-MIL 88A’s good compatibility with CA. The Fe – MOF crystallinity structure has formed a favourable crystal structure on the composite film, enhancing mechanical support and structural stability. Further increase in ANE does not affect the crystallinity of the Fe–MOF/CA matrix.
## 3.5. TGA analysis
The thermal stability of the composite is analyzed using the TGA technique. Fig. 6 illustrates DTG (derivative thermogravimetric) and TGA curves of F1 and Fe-MIL 88A impregnated F2 and Fe-MIL 88A with antibacterial agent blended film F3.
The first degradation stage of CA (F1) film was between 30 and $190^{\circ}\mathsf C,$ , with a weight loss of $48\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . The first degradation of Fe-MIL 88A incorporated film (F2) was observed between room temperature and $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(13~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , and the first degradation of antibacterial agent blended film (F3) was obtained between room temperature that is 30 to $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a weight loss of $4\mathrm{wt\%}$ . The probable reason may be due to the loss of water molecules absorbed in the prepared films, as evidenced by the exothermic DTA peak.
The second degradation occurred at temperatures ranging from 190 to $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for F1, F2 and F3 films $(51.5~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , $100{-}290\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(20.2~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , and $100{-}300\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(18.7~\mathrm{wt\%})$ , which correspond to the acetyl groups decomposition and cellulose backbones respectively. Similar results have been previously reported in the literature [36].
The third decomposition of the organic ligand present in the pre­ pared CA/Fe-MIL 88A and CA/Fe-MIL 88A/ANE blended films occurred at the range $290-390\ {^\circ}\mathrm{C}$ $(54.8~\mathrm{wt\%})$ ), $300{\-}500\ {\mathrm{~}}^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ $(46.7~\mathrm{wt\%})$ which corresponds to the decomposition of compounds present in the prepared films such as CA (F1) and CA/Fe-MIL 88A (F2) and CA/Fe-MIL 88A/ANE (F3) thereafter remained stable up to $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Previous literature had observed similar results [37]. Table 2 shows the thermogravimetric curves of CA (F1) and Fe-MIL 88A and antibacterial agent-coated F2 and F3 films respectively. The comparison of our TGA results with those reported in recent literature reveals the beneficial effect of adding Fe - MIL 88A which improved the thermal stability of F2 and F3 films.
## 3.6. Water contact angle analysis
Water contact angle (WCA) measurements were used to determine the surface hydrophobicity and wettability of films. The hydrophilic and hydrophobic nature of the material is described by surface wettability character. Fig. 7 shows the WCA results for (a) F1 (b) Fe-MIL 88A (c) F2 (d) F3 (e) F4 and (f) F5.
The contact angle degrees are $68.6^{\circ}$ , $84.5^{\circ}$ , $75.9^{\circ}$ , $53.7^{\circ},48.7^{\circ}$ and
$46.5^{\circ}$ for F1, Fe-MIL 88A, F2, F3, F4 and F5 respectively. The contact angle of the CA/Fe-MIL 88A composite film was $75.9^{\circ}$ , which was slightly more hydrophobic than the bare CA film $(68.6^{\circ})$ . But the water absorbed by F3, F4 and F5 films spread on the substrate, thus making it hydrophilic. That is, with increased content of ANE, the WCA value declined from $75.9^{\circ}$ to $46.5^{\circ}$ . The probable reason may be due to the hydrophilic nature of ANE which increases the hydrophilic nature of all composite films.
## 3.7. Water vapour permeability
The Water vapour permeability (WVP) test was used to determine whether the moisture could easily permeate and flow through the ma­ terial. Fig. 8 shows the WVP results of composite films. The WVP of CA film was $0.3267\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mm}/(\mathrm{kPa}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{m}^{2})$ while it slightly increased to $_{0.4311\ g}$ $\mathrm{mm}/(\mathrm{kPa}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{m}^{2})$ for CA blend Fe-MIL 88A film. With increasing ANE content, the WVP values varied from 1.2913 to $1.7562\ \mathrm{g\mm}/(\mathrm{kPa\h}$ m2), but the hydrophilicity of Fe-MIL 88A in composite films did not change. It may be due to the hydrophilic nature of Fe-MIL 88A, which prefers to absorb water molecules even though ANE was present. The porous Fe-MIL 88A nano cage served as a water vapour channel in this investigation, and the addition of more ANE-Fe MIL 88A created more water vapour channels that were far more suitable for water vapour transfer. The insertion of ANE into the Fe-MIL 88A structure led to a still dramatic increase in the WVP of the composite material.
## 3.8. Transparency of films
Table 3 shows the thickness of each film used for calculating the opacity according to Eq. (2). While F1 film, with a T value of 8.86, was the least transparent (highly opacity), followed by those incorporated with Fe-MIL 88A and ANE (Table 2) of F2, F3, F4, and F5 films with the obtained T values of 5.64, 0.85, 0.55, and 0.28, respectively. The opacity of the film samples significantly decreased with increasing ANE con­ centration. An increase in film opacity as a consequence of the addition of ANE has also been reported in fish gelatin films containing borage extract. The addition of Fe-MIL 88A/antibacterial agent to the CA film improved the film’s transparency.
## 3.9. Antibacterial test
Fig. 9 shows the inhibitory effect of F1, Fe-MIL 88A, F2, F3, F4, and F5 against common bacterial strains such as (Escherichia coli) E. coli and Staphylococcus aureus (S. aureus). The inhibitory effect was computed based on the disc and clear solution zone measurements. The inhibitory effect was absent in F1 and F2 films. When compared to F1 and F2, the inhibitory zones for F3, F4, and F5 films against E. coli and S. aureus were much higher. Accordingly, incorporating ANE in Fe-CA/MIL88A films (F3, F4, F5) enhances antibacterial activity. Furthermore, the irradiated ANE also displayed good antibacterial activity for S. aureus (Fig. 9). The
F3, F4 and F5 exhibited superior antimicrobial activities compared to F2 and F1 and could be a promising packaging material for food preservation.
The inhibition zone of modified films against the food-pathogenic bacteria, gram-positive (S. aureus) and gram-negative (E. coli) is shown in Table 4.
## 3.10. Biodegradation test
The degradation rate of different composing films, F1, F2, F3, F4, and F5 was monitored for 90 days in soil. The setup’s atmospheric state was kept constant. The weight loss percentage for each film can be seen in Fig. 10. The weight loss percentage was correlated with the original film. According to the findings, pure F1 film degraded after 60 days $(50\mathrm{wt\%})$ , whereas Fe-MIL 88A integrated film F2 degraded $30~\mathrm{wt\%}$ , and F3, F4, and F5 films showed degradation of 65, 73 and $89\ \mathrm{wt}\%$ , respectively. When compared to F1 and F2 film, the $\%$ weight loss of F3, F4, and F5 film was significantly lower. As a result, the prepared films are biode­ gradable. The addition of the ANE to the CA/Fe-MIL 88 A does not alter the biodegradability of the biocomposite films, making them appro­ priate for being put back in the environment without causing adverse effects.
## 3.11. Real time application
The antibacterial efficiency of the composite films was tested to protect cucumber pieces for food packaging. As shown in Fig. S1. fresh cucumber pieces with increased water content were covered with different composite films (F1, F3, F4, F5) and kept for five days at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with $50\%$ relative humidity. The cucumber piece covered with F1 film appeared dark brown with microbes on the surface, even the next day. The addition of an antibacterial agent as in the case of films F3 and F4 showed slight water loss and a brownish appearance. While film with 4 mL ANE/Fe-MIL 88A/CA (F5) looked relatively fresh with minimal water loss and is free from bacterial attack.
## 4. Conclusion
Cellulose acetate-based composite films were made in this study with
Fe-MIL 88A as the nanocarrier to load Allmania nodiflora extract. The hydrophilicity of Fe-MIL 88A improved the compatibility of Allmania nodiflora extract inside the CA/Fe-MIL 88A matrix substantially. The incorporation of Fe-MIL 88A into the film greatly enhanced their me­ chanical strength, water vapour permeability and transparency. The addition of Allmania nodiflora extract up to $6~\mathrm{mL}$ exhibited effective antibacterial activity against $E.$ . coli and S. aureus. Further addition of an antibacterial agent, beyond the optimum level, decreased the mechan­ ical properties of the composite film. Fresh cucumber pieces covered with F5 composite films looked better, relatively fresh with minimal water loss and without bacterial contamination on comparing with F1, F3 and F4 composite films. This work provides a novel strategy for the preparation of antibacterial food packaging film.
